<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Xiangyang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1267">1267</a>–<a href="/years/1273">1273</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-conquest-of-the-song-dynasty">Mongol conquest of the Song dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> was a long siege of the twin cities of Xiangyang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fancheng">Fancheng</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> from 1267 to 1273. Mongol forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> eventually took them using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counterweight-trebuchet">counterweight trebuchets</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> treat the fall of Xiangyang as the pivotal event that opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> to the Mongol conquest of the Southern Song.</p></div>
